fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1036", description: some(value: "Decide whether WI-1026's rule-body spec-op dispatch should also cover a BUILTIN-MAPPED defaulted spec op, and build the driver that can observe the answer. WI-1026 routes a rule-body call on a defaulted spec op through 'check_apply_iter' so it pins the carrier's supplied impl (or refuses a tie), gated by 'is_defaulted_spec_op', whose first clause is '!kb.is_builtin(f)'. THAT CLAUSE IS THE WHOLE BLAST RADIUS AND IT WAS MEASURED: with it the arm fires on ZERO sites in the corpus (stdlib+bindings+testcases+examples+anthill-todo); WITHOUT it, on 60 — every one a 'PartialOrd.gt/gte/lt/lte' rule-body goal, newly pinned to 'Int64.gt' / 'Float.gt'. The corpus loads clean either way. IT WAS EXCLUDED FOR TWO REASONS, ONE OF WHICH IS THE POINT OF THIS TICKET. (1) It is inert for WI-1026's own path: a builtin is decided by the interpreter's builtin table, and 'reduce_op_value' returns early on a builtin BEFORE it reads a pin — so classifying these changes nothing the ticket was about. (2) It is NOT inert elsewhere: 'req_insertion::run' emits a dispatch rewrite per classified occurrence ('record_apply_rewrite', spec op -> impl op), so widening emits 60 new rewrites. AND THE SUITE CANNOT DECIDE IT — the full workspace is green BOTH WAYS, 4295/0 with the clause and 4295/0 without, so those 60 rewrites are observed by NO test and a green run is not evidence for landing them. WORK, in this order: build a test that OBSERVES an emitted dispatch rewrite for a rule-body call (there is none today — that absence is the finding); then measure the 60 sites old-vs-new through it; then decide. Deleting the clause is one line, but doing it on a green-both-ways suite would be landing an unobserved behaviour change. ACCEPTANCE: either the clause goes with a driver that fails when it is restored, or it stays with the reason written at the site and here. REFERENCE: WI-1026; 'is_defaulted_spec_op' in kb/typing.rs carries the same numbers."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-07T18:41:17Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1036", author: "user", content: "SAFETY CLAIM CORRECTED (/code-review 2026-08-07 finding 3, CONFIRMED against resolve.rs:6143-6147; the doc comment at is_defaulted_spec_op is corrected in the same commit): this ticket recorded blast-radius reasoning rests on an ordering the code does NOT have. reduce_op_value does NOT return early on a builtin before reading a pin — it reads the pin FIRST (op = classified_apply_target().unwrap_or(functor), resolve.rs:6143) and keys the builtin early-return on the PINNED op. So classifying the 60 PartialOrd.gt/gte/lt/lte sites puts the pin in front of the builtin check, which then asks about the impl symbol (Int64.gt / Float.gt) instead of the spelled spec op (PartialOrd.gt). Both happen to be builtin-mapped, so the outcomes may coincide — but that equivalence is a MEASUREMENT this ticket must make, not an ordering to assume; the resolver path IS affected, contrary to the classifying-them-changes-nothing sentence. SEQUENCING: land WI-1042 first — its shared defaulted_spec_op_parent helper is the gate all sites should key on, and its non-operation early-out is the type_params_of_sort cost fix this ticket description says wants fixing FIRST.", at: "2026-08-07T10:52:55Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1036", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED — DECISION REVERSED MID-TICKET BY DRIVING, and the reversal is the finding. First conclusion (written, then refuted by /code-review high): keep the clause, because classifying the builtin-mapped family buys nothing — the pin's only readers are reduce_op_value and eval's apply, and PartialOrd.gt / Int64.gt / Float.gt are ALL registered builtins so the early-return fires either way. That is true of the STDLIB family and FALSE of the population, and it missed what the clause actually withheld. DRIVEN, both halves failing when the clause is restored: (1) the 058 sec 3.7 TIE REFUSAL — a carrier supplying 'gt' twice (own member + WI-431 instance fact) was REFUSED at load from an operation body and ACCEPTED from a rule body; one program, two verdicts, keyed on where the call is written; without the clause the rule body refuses at its own location with the same message. (2) sec 3.1 DISPATCH BY VALUE IN OPERAND POSITION — 'unify(?r, PartialOrd.gt(p,q))' on a carrier with a supplied override answered an INDEFINITE RESIDUAL; classified, the pin names the override and reduce_op_value folds it to false. A user carrier's own gt is an ordinary bodied operation, so the pin IS readable there. BOTH OF THE TICKET'S STATED REASONS WERE FALSE: reason 1 by its own feedback (the pin is read BEFORE the builtin early-return); reason 2 by driving — a rule-body classification emits NO dispatch rewrite at all (req_insertion::run walks kb.op_bodies_iter(); one classified call emits 1 from an operation body and 0 from a rule body), so the '60 new rewrites' could not happen. THE 60 DOES NOT REPRODUCE: four newly decided sites on a stdlib+bindings load (2 gt, 1 lt, 1 gte, all stdlib rule bodies), five with anthill-testcases, none in examples or anthill-todo, counted at the acting arm of dispatch_calls_in_occ. All four are GOAL-position calls pinned to Int64.gt/Float.gt, both builtins — the corpus gets the refusal, not a dispatch change. RESIDUAL, PINNED WITH ITS OWNER: the same call as a rule-body GOAL still answers [] where the operation body answers the override; deleting the clause does not fix it (measured) — a goal takes BuiltinTag::Gt off its SPELLED functor, no goal-position reader consults a pin, and builtin_cmp fails silently on two entities. WI-879 owns it. ALSO: is_defaulted_spec_op is folded away (it was the gate plus the clause); WI-1042's body-test control re-measured after the deletion, same 15 tests. Full workspace 4326/0.", at: "2026-08-07T18:41:05Z")
